00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:10.360
Well, welcome everyone to
the 1:55 showing of "Ant-Man

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:11.770
and the Wasp."

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:14.770
We should-- (LAUGHING)
so thanks for coming out

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:16.280
to the [INAUDIBLE] here.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.640
So this is "Proactively
Restocking Inventory

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.030
Using Machine Learning."

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:21.150
My name is Sandeep Parikh.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:25.010
I am part of the Google Cloud
Solutions Architecture Team.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.071
And so with that, why
don't we get started?

00:00:27.071 --> 00:00:28.570
So what we're going
to cover today--

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:31.600
first I want to walk
through a basic challenge

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.050
that we see often in
retail and in inventory--

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.930
keeping that stock out.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:39.940
I want to walk through a
few of the modern approaches

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.710
that people tend
to take when trying

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:43.815
to solve inventory challenges.

00:00:43.815 --> 00:00:45.940
And then we'll figure out
how we can build a system

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.584
to solve for this problem.

00:00:47.584 --> 00:00:49.000
What are some of
the components we

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.930
can use to address the
inventory scenario?

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:56.155
And we'll map that back to the
components inside of Google

00:00:56.155 --> 00:00:58.450
Cloud Platform and what
an architecture might

00:00:58.450 --> 00:00:59.620
look like in production.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.661
And I'm hoping to showcase
a little bit of a demo

00:01:01.661 --> 00:01:03.050
as well before we
get to the end.

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.170
And then finally, I want
to close on questions.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:06.670
And just so you
know, there are mics

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:09.640
at the ends of both of the
third or fourth aisle here.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.890
If not, if you can't get
out there, just try to shout

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:15.274
and I'll repeat it so that
others can hear it as well.

00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:16.690
So what's the
problem we're trying

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:20.040
to solve in this example?

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:20.710
It's produce.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.750
Bananas, in particular.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.420
So specifically what
we're trying to do

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:28.170
is keep bananas in
stock on the floor.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:29.536
On a grocery store floor--

00:01:29.536 --> 00:01:30.910
you can imagine
a produce section

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:32.440
inside of a larger store.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.030
We're trying to
keep that in stock.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:36.490
Now we can't leave too
many out on the floor,

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:38.950
because obviously
they'll go bad.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:41.440
But if we wait too
long to restock them,

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:42.700
we'll lose sales, right?

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.950
And you also risk that
kind of inventory loss

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.390
by leaving it in the back
room for too long as well.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:50.710
So this is a pretty
high turnover use case

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.560
where a lot of product needs to
move off the floor relatively

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.510
quickly, but we need
to restock it just

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:57.582
as fast to make sure
we're not missing out

00:01:57.582 --> 00:01:59.290
and we don't have to
get rid of anything.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:00.373
This is a perishable item.

00:02:00.373 --> 00:02:03.430
And you can sort of map this
to several different use cases.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.690
But in particular, I think
produce is a good one.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:12.630
It's a good stand-in for a low
margin, high turnover item.

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:15.480
So how do folks typically solve
inventory challenges today?

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.390
And not just produce, but
broadly around inventory

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.630
challenges.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:24.690
So there's typically three main
components in these scenarios--

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:28.460
RFID, cameras, and people.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:30.570
And if we unpack
each one of those,

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.240
we figure out that
it's not a perfect fit

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.520
for this kind of
produce style use case.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.360
So with RFID, there are
typically two main approaches.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.974
You have active tracking
or active scanning,

00:02:42.974 --> 00:02:44.890
and that's where you've
got a handheld scanner

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.470
and you're walking up
and scanning rough areas

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:49.590
of individual items.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.540
The second approach
is passive scanning.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.660
And passive requires you
to install antennas up

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.490
on the walls or in
the ceiling, and then

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.880
you're basically blanketing
an area with scans of RFID,

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.820
and you're waiting for the
responses to come back.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.620
Both of them have
their own challenges,

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.780
and ultimately the
costs don't really

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.410
line up with high
turnover, low margin items.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.580
You're not going to put an
RFID tag on every banana,

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.110
or every head of lettuce,
or every pack of gum that's

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:17.610
in there.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.850
And it also requires a lot
of significant investment

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.350
in both cases.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.270
In terms of the
handheld scanners,

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.010
it's a little bit
more manageable

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.344
because you can actually
outfit several individuals

00:03:28.344 --> 00:03:29.760
and they can spend
their time just

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:33.120
roaming around the space
scanning for items.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.790
With passive tracking, you
don't need to send people out,

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:38.610
but there's a high
cost for the antennas,

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:40.990
and the antenna placement,
and there's also a high cost

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:43.290
to actually correlate
each of the readings

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.390
and figure out what's accurate.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:47.447
For passive tracking,
the accuracy

00:03:47.447 --> 00:03:49.530
is a little bit more
difficult right out the gate,

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:52.170
so it does require an additional
investment to really get

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.735
to the same level of accuracy
that you might get out

00:03:54.735 --> 00:03:56.576
of a handheld scanner.

00:03:56.576 --> 00:03:58.200
But like I said,
ultimately the problem

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.030
is that the costs
don't match up well.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.440
Now what about cameras?

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.280
Cameras are plentiful, right?

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.620
They're pretty inexpensive,
relatively speaking,

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:11.010
especially compared to something
like RFID out the gate,

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.310
and they have pretty
good resolution.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:16.519
And they actually have a dual
purpose, or a multipurpose.

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:18.500
You can use them
for security footage

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:21.120
as well as watching stock.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.060
But there are some caveats.

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.340
So with stock, you're
probably only going

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:28.090
to get an overhead
view of the world.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:30.170
You're not going to get
down to shelf level,

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:32.900
so it's not a great solution
for every single kind

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.344
of stock or inventory use case.

00:04:34.344 --> 00:04:36.260
And again, you can
imagine-- like I'm thinking

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:39.034
of a grocery store in my head.

00:04:39.034 --> 00:04:40.700
But ultimately, the
problem with cameras

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.390
is that they require somebody
actually evaluating the footage

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:45.770
that's coming off of them.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:48.320
You effectively need
someone or something

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.000
monitoring them to
decide what to do.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.059
Which brings us to the
third kind of approach,

00:04:54.059 --> 00:04:55.725
and I think one of
the harder challenges

00:04:55.725 --> 00:04:58.380
as well, which is people.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.220
In both cases, if you've
got active RFID tracking

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:04.500
or you've got
cameras deployed, you

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.780
need people there to actually
administer and operate

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:10.770
this mechanism, and
people are hard to scale.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.870
You've got to hire a
fair amount of people

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.990
to get folks turnover, or
get enough coverage in terms

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:18.390
of holding the scanners,
or watching the camera

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:21.396
footage across a number
of different cameras.

00:05:21.396 --> 00:05:22.520
They're also hard to train.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:25.592
You're not going to keep
training more and more people.

00:05:25.592 --> 00:05:27.050
But ultimately,
the biggest problem

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.540
is that you end up with
a lot of wasted cycles.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:31.550
You end up having
a lot of people who

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:33.410
might just be standing around.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:35.127
And as much as I
would love to be

00:05:35.127 --> 00:05:36.710
paid to stare at
produce all day long,

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:38.210
it's not terribly exciting.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:46.110
So one of the things we can do
is we can make smarter cameras.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:47.360
That's a great way to do this.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:48.943
Now we have a camera
on the front end,

00:05:48.943 --> 00:05:51.020
and we have a pretty
powerful brain behind it.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:53.030
And that's how we can
solve this problem.

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:55.850
And really what you get down to
is a pretty simple technology

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:59.060
on the front end for capturing
the data that we need

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.600
to gather, but we still need a
scalable technology behind it

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:04.080
to analyze that footage.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.260
So we laid out the challenge.

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:10.190
We've covered a little
bit of the approach.

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:12.470
Now we know what our
challenge is, right?

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:14.630
So let's figure out,
what are the pieces we

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:16.620
need to make this
work at a high level?

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.570
Let's talk about the
major pillars here.

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:21.480
So there's five major pieces.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:22.657
The first are cameras.

00:06:22.657 --> 00:06:23.990
That's kind of a solved problem.

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:26.960
We've figured out that
those are going to be there.

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:29.049
We also need a scalable
storage mechanism

00:06:29.049 --> 00:06:30.590
to store the footage
that we're going

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:32.970
to pull off of those cameras.

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:35.880
Then we need some kind of
a classification mechanism.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:39.780
Images, video-- something to
put that brain behind the camera

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:40.934
footage.

00:06:40.934 --> 00:06:43.100
And then we actually have
to finish this up and say,

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:46.010
how do we notify a store
associate or a team member

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:49.850
on the floor and let them know
that they've got to go restock

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:51.757
the item in question?

00:06:51.757 --> 00:06:54.340
And then the final piece in the
middle, there's orchestration.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:56.820
How do we get this whole
machine humming and running

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.360
and operating on
a regular basis?

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.200
So the bottom four
components are the ones

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.240
that we can do inside
of Google Cloud.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:09.660
So you can imagine you've got
cameras deployed at the store.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:12.370
Those need to actually push
those images up to a storage.

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:14.100
The classifier will
pick up those images,

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.842
classify them, figure
out what's in them,

00:07:15.842 --> 00:07:18.300
and then based on what they
see, they'll send a message out

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.320
to the team member.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:21.819
So all that we can do, and that
orchestration component that

00:07:21.819 --> 00:07:24.080
runs it all, all of that
can run inside of GCP today.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.310
So just to quickly recap here.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:29.780
Cameras are plentiful.

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:33.327
They can scale up from consumer
class to commercial class.

00:07:33.327 --> 00:07:35.660
You can find them in all
manner of different resolutions

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:37.160
or black and white or color.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:38.744
You can get really
cool fish eye ones.

00:07:38.744 --> 00:07:40.993
Ones that are mounted on the
ceiling and pan and zoom.

00:07:40.993 --> 00:07:42.540
There is an endless
array of cameras.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:43.040
Trust me.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:46.260
I looked before the talk, just
to see what was out there.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:49.550
And you actually have the
option of streaming or sampling

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:50.930
that data.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:53.240
The thing to remember
here is that if you

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:55.880
imagine a scenario where
you've got hundreds of stores,

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:59.330
thousands of stores streaming
video off of every security

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:02.740
camera, back up to a
cloud infrastructure--

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:05.140
technically speaking,
not terribly

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:08.500
difficult. But from a bandwidth
and cost perspective, again,

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:10.270
you're getting into
that equation of,

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:12.790
do the costs line up with
what I'm trying to solve for?

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:15.490
So you may not necessarily
want to over-invest

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:17.380
in network bandwidth
to stream video

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:21.310
when sampling every minute
with a two-megapixel image

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:23.919
could be just as sufficient.

00:08:23.919 --> 00:08:25.710
Once you get into the
cloud side of things,

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:26.918
that's where we need storage.

00:08:26.918 --> 00:08:29.310
We need a scalable
object storage platform

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:32.590
to store this data coming in.

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:35.909
So we would have the
camera upload that data.

00:08:35.909 --> 00:08:38.880
And you need a little bit of
local device or a control plane

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:39.380
there.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:42.659
Something that's
on the store side

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:44.520
to capture and
sample the images,

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:45.990
package them up,
and then send them

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:48.150
to the cloud infrastructure.

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:50.310
And that local device
can be anything from--

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.400
I've seen people deploy things
like single board computers,

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:57.390
like a Raspberry Pi, all the
way up to rack mount servers.

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:00.930
It really just depends on the
size and scope and the number

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:02.220
of cameras you've got there.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:04.710
And effectively how often
you're sampling, as well.

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:09.510
All right, what
about a classifier?

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.590
It turns out building
an image classifier is

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:15.040
a non-trivial exercise.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.130
You need a lot of data science
expertise and understanding

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:21.820
to train a model, and to
build and train a model,

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:24.550
and then you need a fair
amount of ops expertise

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:26.650
to really deploy
and scale that model

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:29.530
and run it for a production
use case like this.

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:31.990
Again, think of the
model of a lot of cameras

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:34.540
across a large store,
a lot of team members,

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:36.947
and now multiply
that by 100 or 1,000.

00:09:36.947 --> 00:09:39.280
That's a lot it's got to
process through and understand,

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:43.030
so it's going to have to be very
scalable and production-ready.

00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:45.330
Well, how can we make this
easier on the Google Cloud

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:48.280
side?

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:50.580
That's where the Cloud
Vision API comes in.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:55.860
The Vision API-- imagine this
as a turnkey pre-trained model.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:57.450
All you have to do
is give it images,

00:09:57.450 --> 00:09:59.670
and what it gives back
to you are everything

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:00.850
we find in the image.

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:04.400
That could be any kind
of classification,

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:07.345
any sort of object
detection, face detection.

00:10:07.345 --> 00:10:09.446
It can actually
read words as well.

00:10:09.446 --> 00:10:09.945
It does OCR.

00:10:09.945 --> 00:10:12.180
It has OCR capabilities.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.176
So anything you would need
to pull out of an image

00:10:15.176 --> 00:10:17.550
is possible with Vision API--
anything you need to learn.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.380
And it can do a lot
of complicated things.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:22.560
Like for example, if you
have two objects in there,

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:23.860
it'll tell you the color.

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:25.734
It'll tell you the
percentage of those colors

00:10:25.734 --> 00:10:27.300
as they occur within that image.

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:29.020
So you have a lot
of capability here,

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:33.640
and it's a really easy
API to get started with.

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:35.440
But what if that's
not enough either?

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:37.137
You could imagine
a scenario where--

00:10:37.137 --> 00:10:38.970
well, what if you have
a very particular use

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:40.860
case, or a very
particular set up,

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:43.800
and you need a little bit more
than just what the stock Vision

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:46.410
API gives you?

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:49.580
And for that, we actually
have something called AutoML.

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:54.270
AutoML is an effort that started
out of the Google AI team--

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:56.870
the research team
inside of Google.

00:10:56.870 --> 00:10:59.884
And their focus was
really on building machine

00:10:59.884 --> 00:11:00.550
learning models.

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:02.260
And I didn't expect
you to be able to read

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:04.009
this text, by the way,
but apparently it's

00:11:04.009 --> 00:11:06.640
probably somewhat legible.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:08.860
But their focus was
really on saying, look.

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:12.350
Building machine learning models
requires a lot of manual work.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:12.970
Right?

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:15.299
How can we make
this process easier?

00:11:15.299 --> 00:11:16.840
Well, we know that
when you're trying

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.510
to build something inside
of even a narrow use case,

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:20.650
there's still a lot of--

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:22.630
I wouldn't say
boilerplate, but there

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:26.890
are some known permutations
that are certainly possible.

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:30.670
Well, what if we could build
a neural network to generate

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:32.570
a machine learning model?

00:11:32.570 --> 00:11:33.910
And that's what AutoML is.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:39.780
So we've taken AutoML and
we've applied it to Vision.

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:43.980
And now what we can do is you
provide labeled image data

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:46.060
to us.

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:48.960
We can actually build a
machine learning model

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:50.310
and give that back to you.

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:51.810
And so it's a machine
learning model

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:54.570
that's trained on
your image data,

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:56.700
and it's custom to
you specifically.

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:58.470
It's not shared amongst
any other projects

00:11:58.470 --> 00:11:59.520
or any other customers.

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:02.490
It's dedicated to
just your use case.

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:05.610
And what's even better is the
same serving infrastructure

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:09.780
that serves the Cloud Vision
API serves your AutoML model.

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:12.360
So that means when you make your
Vision API call, by default,

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:14.539
it'll use the standard
model we've built for you.

00:12:14.539 --> 00:12:16.080
But you actually
have an option there

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:17.582
to change it to a custom model.

00:12:17.582 --> 00:12:20.040
And you can just highlight the
custom model and the version

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:21.540
you want to use,
and it will actually

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:23.164
use your machine
learning model that we

00:12:23.164 --> 00:12:25.700
generated on your behalf.

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:27.290
And what's even
better is if you don't

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:29.030
have labeled images, that's OK.

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:31.500
We can actually help annotate
them for you as well.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:32.960
So we have a lot
of the capability

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:35.720
that you need to build
something very specific.

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:40.400
Now what I will often tell
people in the real world is,

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:44.130
the stock Vision API might get
you 60%, 70%, 80% of the way,

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:45.784
and there might
be that last 20%.

00:12:45.784 --> 00:12:47.450
But let's figure out
what that is first.

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:49.700
So don't invest too
much time if you

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:51.270
don't have images quite yet.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:52.830
Let's try the
Vision API as it is,

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:55.330
and if that's not a good fit,
then we can move on to AutoML.

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:00.560
So if you look at the
breakdown of usage

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:03.160
in a really simple scenario
here, I've got images

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:05.410
and I want to pass them
through a classifier that's

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:06.520
very capable.

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:09.810
On the bottom I've got
the stock Vision API.

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:12.010
And on the top I've got
the AutoML Vision API.

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:13.510
And again, the only
distinction here

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:16.780
is that AutoML Vision needed
one additional input, which

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:18.970
was a bunch of labeled
images up front.

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:21.520
After that, they look,
feel, and function

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:22.540
in exactly the same way.

00:13:26.259 --> 00:13:28.300
All right, so what about
the messaging component?

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:33.100
How do we notify an associate
or a team member on the floor

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:35.260
that they've got to go restock?

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:37.560
Well, that's where
Firebase comes in.

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:41.940
Firebase is our complete
mobile backend as a service.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:44.320
And I'm not going to go through
all of what Firebase has,

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:47.390
but imagine a system
for authentication,

00:13:47.390 --> 00:13:51.800
complete SDKs for
Android and iOS,

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:55.000
and it actually supports
web applications as well.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.890
Gives you real time
database synchronization

00:13:56.890 --> 00:13:57.890
behind the scenes.

00:13:57.890 --> 00:13:59.330
So it's got a whole
bunch of functionality,

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:01.455
and I haven't even touched
on a good portion of it,

00:14:01.455 --> 00:14:03.010
but a whole mess
of functionality

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:05.426
that you can use to build
mobile applications.

00:14:05.426 --> 00:14:07.300
And part of those-- one
of those key features

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:09.880
that we need to leverage
in this case, is messaging.

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:12.080
So built-in push
messaging support,

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:13.330
and that's what I'll use here.

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:15.102
And actually, that
push messaging

00:14:15.102 --> 00:14:17.560
also supports the web, which
I will show a little bit later

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:18.570
in my demo for you.

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:23.960
All right, so we've gotten
the four main pillars

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:25.010
on the outside, right?

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:28.760
Containers, storage,
the classifier,

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:31.240
the messaging component.

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:32.740
What about orchestration?

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:35.510
How do we actually make
this thing run together?

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:37.720
And it turns out it's a
handful of components.

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:38.680
Not just one.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:44.110
But the core of it is some kind
of an application platform,

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:49.450
whether that's Cloud Functions,
App Engine, Kubernetes Engine,

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:50.266
or Compute Engine.

00:14:50.266 --> 00:14:51.640
You've got four
main options here

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:53.848
that we need to run some
kind of a compute mechanism,

00:14:53.848 --> 00:14:56.320
or some sort of set of
applications and services

00:14:56.320 --> 00:14:58.970
to actually shuttle this
stuff all the way through.

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:00.449
Once those images
land in storage,

00:15:00.449 --> 00:15:01.990
they've got to get
to the classifier.

00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:03.573
Once they get to the
classifier, we've

00:15:03.573 --> 00:15:05.050
got to parse those
results back out

00:15:05.050 --> 00:15:06.580
and decide if we
want to message,

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:10.245
and then keep that
whole machine running.

00:15:10.245 --> 00:15:11.870
So I won't go into
too much depth here.

00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:14.651
There have been a number
of sessions over yesterday

00:15:14.651 --> 00:15:16.400
and probably some more
today and tomorrow,

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:18.770
around the different
Compute Platforms,

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:21.069
but at an incredibly high level.

00:15:21.069 --> 00:15:22.610
Cloud Functions
gives you the ability

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:25.730
to run serverless functions,
just like you'd expect.

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:29.530
And those can be either
in Node or Python.

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:33.460
App Engine lets you build more
full-blown apps and services

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:35.530
and let us help you
scale them automatically

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:38.430
based on incoming traffic.

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:40.170
Kubernetes Engine
is a level deeper

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:41.961
than App Engine, where
you have the ability

00:15:41.961 --> 00:15:43.890
to basically run
containers and manage them

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:46.364
at scale across a larger
number of machines.

00:15:46.364 --> 00:15:48.780
This is where the applications
might get more complicated.

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:51.490
They may have more
features and functionality.

00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:53.670
And then finally we
give you really fast VMs

00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:55.300
in Compute Engine.

00:15:55.300 --> 00:15:57.650
So you have a few
different options here,

00:15:57.650 --> 00:16:02.020
and I'll tell you I think
depending on the use case,

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:04.330
one or another might
make the most sense.

00:16:04.330 --> 00:16:08.420
I'm happy to talk through with
any one of you about which one

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:09.630
is the right choice.

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:12.310
But for most production
applications, most production

00:16:12.310 --> 00:16:17.020
deployments, we often see App
Engine or Kubernetes Engine,

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:20.020
because they have a fair
amount of capability

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:22.120
and a lot of knobs
that you can still

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:24.250
turn to make things work
the way you want them to,

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:26.730
without giving you too much
on the Compute Engine side

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:29.050
and maybe not being too
limiting like you might

00:16:29.050 --> 00:16:30.801
run into with Cloud Functions.

00:16:33.582 --> 00:16:35.290
All right, so let's
put this all together

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:36.920
and see what it looks like.

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:40.840
So the biggest thing I would
say for a typical production use

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:45.700
case is with AutoML
Vision, a trained

00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:49.104
model based on your images
and your image data.

00:16:49.104 --> 00:16:50.770
And then either App
Engine or Kubernetes

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:53.244
Engine, and using Firebase.

00:16:53.244 --> 00:16:54.910
So those are really
the four [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:59.440
components that we need to use.

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:03.210
So if we dig into an actual
rough production architecture

00:17:03.210 --> 00:17:05.760
that I would typically suggest
you would use in this case,

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:10.520
what we have about 100 feet
up from me on the left there--

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:11.760
capture and upload.

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:13.829
So that's capturing
those images.

00:17:13.829 --> 00:17:16.528
Capturing that video
in a store environment.

00:17:16.528 --> 00:17:18.569
That little control plane
I talked about earlier,

00:17:18.569 --> 00:17:21.480
that local device that's
going to pull those images off

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:24.020
and actually package them up
and send them to the Cloud,

00:17:24.020 --> 00:17:25.950
that's that little
upload device there.

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:28.200
And then it lands inside of GCP.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:29.820
And once it gets
into GCP, you've

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:32.220
got a handful of
boxes and components

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:34.480
here to really drive
this and make it happen.

00:17:34.480 --> 00:17:37.470
So the first thing we do
is those images land inside

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:39.300
of Google Cloud Storage.

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:41.850
And Cloud Storage actually has
this really awesome feature

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:44.610
called Bucket Change
Notifications.

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:48.360
And what that means is every
time you upload an object,

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:51.730
or you delete an object, or you
change metadata in an object,

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:53.730
it can actually generate
notifications for you--

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:55.350
generate messages for you.

00:17:55.350 --> 00:17:58.380
And those messages can go to a
number of downstream targets.

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:01.170
The two most popular are
either Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:05.040
our messaging infrastructure,
or Cloud Functions.

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:06.930
In this case, I
chose Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:08.980
and I'll come back to the
why I made that choice.

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:10.712
But what I've done
here is every time

00:18:10.712 --> 00:18:12.420
a new image is uploaded
into this bucket,

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:15.410
it fires off a bucket
change notification.

00:18:15.410 --> 00:18:17.880
That bucket change
notification-- that payload

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:20.940
is actually packaged up
as a Cloud Pub/Sub message

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:23.370
and sent to a topic.

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:27.940
That Cloud Pub/Sub topic has
a push subscription on it,

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:29.740
and that push
subscription actually

00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:34.594
can send it right to a target
immediately in real time.

00:18:34.594 --> 00:18:36.010
So in this case,
what I've done is

00:18:36.010 --> 00:18:39.230
I'm showing it going to
a Kubernetes Engine app.

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:41.230
Just a service that I'm
running in there called,

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:42.760
let's say, Process Image.

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:44.590
That Process Image
service is actually

00:18:44.590 --> 00:18:47.002
going to make the call to
the AutoML Vision service.

00:18:47.002 --> 00:18:48.460
It's going to pass
the image along.

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:50.001
Actually what it's
going to do is not

00:18:50.001 --> 00:18:51.300
pass the actual physical image.

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:54.580
It's going to pass a
Cloud Storage bucket

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:55.850
location of the image.

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:58.420
So I can just say, here's an
image that's in Cloud Storage.

00:18:58.420 --> 00:18:59.540
Please analyze it for me.

00:18:59.540 --> 00:19:02.450
And it's going to give
me back a JSON response.

00:19:02.450 --> 00:19:04.360
I'm going to run through
that JSON response,

00:19:04.360 --> 00:19:05.920
look for what I need to--

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:09.350
any kind of restock message
that I want to have in there.

00:19:09.350 --> 00:19:12.130
And then if we need
to restock something,

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:14.560
that process image service
will send another message back

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:17.120
out to a different
Pub/Sub topic, which

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:19.700
has a push subscription
to another service

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:21.770
inside of Kubernetes
Engine, and that

00:19:21.770 --> 00:19:23.720
will decide to actually
make the connection

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:25.740
to Firebase and say, go
ahead and send a push

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:30.270
message to whichever devices are
appropriate to get the message.

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:32.550
So it's a little bit of
a back and forth here.

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:34.352
But I did this for
a couple of reasons.

00:19:34.352 --> 00:19:35.810
You could actually
orchestrate this

00:19:35.810 --> 00:19:37.740
with probably a couple
of Cloud Functions

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:39.860
and a little bit smaller scope.

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:43.790
But again, go back to
the original example.

00:19:43.790 --> 00:19:46.070
Pretty big store
with lots of cameras.

00:19:46.070 --> 00:19:48.200
A lot of stores.

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:50.310
There's going to be a
lot of data generated off

00:19:50.310 --> 00:19:51.480
of those images, right?

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:52.890
A lot of images
getting uploaded.

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:56.844
A lot of potential data
getting analyzed there.

00:19:56.844 --> 00:19:59.010
So I picked a Pub/Sub for
a particular reason, which

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:03.150
is if you want to have the
ability to fan out and say,

00:20:03.150 --> 00:20:05.970
well I want to analyze
a lot of these events,

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:08.700
or I want to persist each
of these incoming events

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:11.280
as maybe a time series
for a later analysis.

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:13.500
Or I want to drive a
bunch of other maybe

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:17.670
downstream workflows based on
images being uploaded, or based

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:21.060
on messages being sent,
before they get sent out.

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:23.700
You have the ability to do
that with Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:25.440
A single topic in
Pub/Sub can actually

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:28.650
have multiple
subscriptions, push or pull.

00:20:28.650 --> 00:20:30.900
So that means I could have
different applications that

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:33.300
are going to take
action on the message,

00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:35.700
or I could have different
applications like a Dataflow

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:38.490
pipeline that's analyzing the
data coming out of that Pub/Sub

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:39.814
topic.

00:20:39.814 --> 00:20:41.730
So it gives you a lot
of power and flexibility

00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:43.920
to change components and
swap them in and out,

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:45.591
or add more or less.

00:20:45.591 --> 00:20:47.590
So Pub/Sub up is a really,
really powerful tool,

00:20:47.590 --> 00:20:49.824
and I don't think it
gets nearly enough love.

00:20:49.824 --> 00:20:51.990
I think we should talk about
it a lot more, in fact,

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:54.120
because it's incredibly capable.

00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:56.280
It's this really,
really powerful glue,

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:59.100
and you can add
lots of subscribers

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:01.590
onto it with literally
no impact on performance.

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:05.240
No change for the most part.

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:07.460
So this is an example
of a production approach

00:21:07.460 --> 00:21:09.860
that we might take.

00:21:09.860 --> 00:21:12.170
Well, as part of this I
wanted to show you guys

00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:14.090
a little bit of
a demo, so I want

00:21:14.090 --> 00:21:19.292
to show you how to put this
together in an example set up.

00:21:19.292 --> 00:21:20.500
So the first thing I needed--

00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:22.125
if we go back to that
bananas challenge

00:21:22.125 --> 00:21:24.791
was I needed some sample
data to test against.

00:21:24.791 --> 00:21:27.040
Somebody sent me this photo
they took a long time ago.

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:28.206
This is me in a banana suit.

00:21:28.206 --> 00:21:30.190
This is actually not
useful sample data

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:33.170
for the test we're
trying to do here.

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:35.900
So not quite what
we're looking for.

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:39.820
So actually, this is the set of
images I used as a basis here.

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:41.620
So I went into a
Google Micro-Kitchen

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:43.420
and I took the bowl
of bananas out.

00:21:43.420 --> 00:21:45.790
I took it back to my desk,
after I got some weird looks

00:21:45.790 --> 00:21:46.940
from a few folks.

00:21:46.940 --> 00:21:48.940
And I wrote this little
note in there that said,

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:50.020
"Restock the bananas."

00:21:50.020 --> 00:21:51.520
And I left it on
the bottom, and you

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:54.717
can see it's poking out a little
bit in the middle image there.

00:21:54.717 --> 00:21:56.300
And I took these
images with my phone,

00:21:56.300 --> 00:21:58.270
so it's not a completely
accurate representation,

00:21:58.270 --> 00:22:00.353
because I've got a pretty
good camera on my phone.

00:22:00.353 --> 00:22:02.650
So I actually scaled them
down to two megapixels

00:22:02.650 --> 00:22:05.229
and turned them black and
white, and that's actually

00:22:05.229 --> 00:22:07.020
a pretty close
representation of what you'd

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:09.070
get out of a common camera.

00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:10.070
Color, black and white--

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:11.800
I think that's just
a minor cost piece.

00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:18.700
But two megapixels was a good
average at a low price point.

00:22:18.700 --> 00:22:20.670
So I started with this,
and what I did was

00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:23.640
I tried to build as much
of the production approach

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:25.140
that I could.

00:22:25.140 --> 00:22:27.270
So with that, let me
switch to my laptop

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:28.610
here and show you guys.

00:22:33.381 --> 00:22:33.880
Perfect.

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:37.400
Does that look
readable to everybody?

00:22:37.400 --> 00:22:37.900
Thumbs?

00:22:37.900 --> 00:22:38.140
OK.

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:38.940
I think we're OK.

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:39.710
We'll go with it.

00:22:39.710 --> 00:22:42.110
All right, so in
this directory here--

00:22:42.110 --> 00:22:44.280
and I've got Cloud
Shell up and running.

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:45.740
So this is inside my project.

00:22:45.740 --> 00:22:48.210
So what I've got are,
I've got nine images.

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:50.780
The bottom three are the
original-- one, two, and three.

00:22:50.780 --> 00:22:52.700
So that was the first,
second, and third.

00:22:52.700 --> 00:22:54.687
The third has the note in it.

00:22:54.687 --> 00:22:56.520
And then I scaled it
down to two megapixels,

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:59.430
and then I did black and white.

00:22:59.430 --> 00:23:01.800
And now I have already
the bucket set up

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:04.455
and the Pub/Sub topic set
up, so if I can show you--

00:23:04.455 --> 00:23:07.440
gsutil-- so I'm going to show
you that the bucket itself

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:08.730
is actually empty right now.

00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:18.830
So we'll see the bucket's empty.

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:21.730
So the first thing I'm
going to do is copy in one

00:23:21.730 --> 00:23:25.960
of the out-of-stock
images, so that's--

00:23:35.150 --> 00:23:35.750
Oh.

00:23:35.750 --> 00:23:36.250
Thank you.

00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:37.942
[CHUCKLING]

00:23:41.639 --> 00:23:42.930
Never type in front of a crowd.

00:23:52.230 --> 00:23:54.390
So what I'm basically
simulating here

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:56.520
is that local device
taking an image

00:23:56.520 --> 00:23:58.660
and pushing it up
to Cloud Storage.

00:23:58.660 --> 00:24:01.550
So I'm going to copy
that image in there.

00:24:01.550 --> 00:24:04.570
And this is one of
those out-of-stock ones.

00:24:04.570 --> 00:24:06.314
So what we should see
in about a second--

00:24:06.314 --> 00:24:08.730
all right, can you see that
little notification down there

00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:09.400
at the bottom?

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:11.050
It says, "Inventory update.

00:24:11.050 --> 00:24:12.940
Time to restock the bananas."

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:14.740
So in that time,
what's happened is

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:17.260
I uploaded the image
to Cloud Storage,

00:24:17.260 --> 00:24:20.910
fired off a bucket change
notification to Pub/Sub,

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:24.760
that Pub/Sub topic delivered a
message via push subscription

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:28.000
to an App Engine app in
this case for demo purposes.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:30.684
That App Engine
app actually looked

00:24:30.684 --> 00:24:32.350
at the payload of
that message and said,

00:24:32.350 --> 00:24:34.070
OK, a new image
has been uploaded.

00:24:34.070 --> 00:24:36.653
I'm going to go ahead and throw
that image to the stock Vision

00:24:36.653 --> 00:24:38.000
API.

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:40.640
Vision API came back with
a response and said, yep,

00:24:40.640 --> 00:24:42.950
I see text in there that
says "restock bananas."

00:24:42.950 --> 00:24:45.241
So I fired off another message
from that App Engine app

00:24:45.241 --> 00:24:47.697
to Pub/Sub and said,
go ahead and restock.

00:24:47.697 --> 00:24:49.280
Another App Engine
app took over there

00:24:49.280 --> 00:24:52.550
and actually made the connection
to Firebase and sent it off.

00:24:52.550 --> 00:24:54.740
So all that happened
in about a second.

00:24:54.740 --> 00:24:57.470
It takes, I think, about a
second for the first service

00:24:57.470 --> 00:25:00.350
and then about a handful
of milliseconds-- or less

00:25:00.350 --> 00:25:02.240
than a second for
the second part.

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:04.130
So the first thing
I want to show you

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:07.270
is what that App Engine service
looks like for the first part--

00:25:07.270 --> 00:25:09.136
so the process image component.

00:25:16.129 --> 00:25:17.420
So that's a little bit legible.

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:20.960
So there's a little bit of
boilerplate up here at the top.

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:24.280
But I want to show that
the purpose here is not

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:26.510
to go through a line-by-line
read of this code,

00:25:26.510 --> 00:25:28.950
but really to tell you it's
not terribly complicated.

00:25:28.950 --> 00:25:30.790
I spent more time
putting, I think,

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:33.027
comments and some
log statements in.

00:25:33.027 --> 00:25:35.110
But really what I'm doing
is just the first thing.

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:37.516
When you set up that push
subscription inside of Pub/Sub,

00:25:37.516 --> 00:25:38.890
you actually give
it an endpoint.

00:25:38.890 --> 00:25:40.764
You say, send the messages
to this end point.

00:25:40.764 --> 00:25:42.370
So your end point
actually doesn't

00:25:42.370 --> 00:25:45.220
have to do anything with
the Pub/Sub mechanism.

00:25:45.220 --> 00:25:46.930
It doesn't have
to go pull from it

00:25:46.930 --> 00:25:48.760
or actually authenticate
to that service.

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:51.550
It just gets that
as post payload,

00:25:51.550 --> 00:25:53.920
right inside that
request function.

00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:55.870
And so now I get inside
the request data,

00:25:55.870 --> 00:25:59.020
and I can actually
turn that payload.

00:25:59.020 --> 00:26:00.670
I can decode it
and I can actually

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:04.540
turn it into a set of JSON and
get the message payload back

00:26:04.540 --> 00:26:05.660
out of it.

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:10.620
And once I do that, all I'm
checking for to make sure is,

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:13.420
is this message coming
from Google Cloud Storage?

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:15.400
Just in case someone
was able to get access,

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:17.650
just want to have a quick
little debug check in there.

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:19.804
But then I get down into,
what am I actually doing?

00:26:19.804 --> 00:26:21.220
So the first thing
I'm going to do

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:23.290
is I'm extracting
the image path--

00:26:23.290 --> 00:26:26.500
the full path of that image
that was just uploaded.

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:29.290
And then I'm actually creating a
really quick call to the Vision

00:26:29.290 --> 00:26:31.240
API, and it's only
a handful of lines,

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:34.030
and you'll see it
starts right down here.

00:26:34.030 --> 00:26:34.780
First thing I do--

00:26:34.780 --> 00:26:37.810
I create a little client
to talk to the Vision API.

00:26:37.810 --> 00:26:40.250
And then I tell it I want
to annotate an image.

00:26:40.250 --> 00:26:43.780
I give it the source location
in Google Cloud Storage,

00:26:43.780 --> 00:26:45.760
and then I ask it
to do two things--

00:26:45.760 --> 00:26:47.470
give me all the
labels, so detect

00:26:47.470 --> 00:26:49.660
all the objects in
the image, and give me

00:26:49.660 --> 00:26:52.540
some labels and some
confidence around those.

00:26:52.540 --> 00:26:55.260
And then let me know if
there's any text in the image.

00:26:55.260 --> 00:26:58.450
So when I get that
payload back, what

00:26:58.450 --> 00:27:01.750
I'm able to do way
down here is actually

00:27:01.750 --> 00:27:04.879
iterate through all of
the label annotations

00:27:04.879 --> 00:27:07.420
and all of the text annotations
that the Vision API gave back

00:27:07.420 --> 00:27:10.480
to me, and figure out,
did it find anything

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:13.710
around "restock bananas?"

00:27:13.710 --> 00:27:16.230
And so once I find that,
yes, it did indeed,

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:18.180
find that text in
the image, then I

00:27:18.180 --> 00:27:20.520
can send a message
to Cloud Pub/Sub

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:22.680
and let the downstream
services that

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:28.390
need to take action-- let them
know that we've got to restock.

00:27:28.390 --> 00:27:33.580
And one interesting note here
is if you set up a Cloud Pub/Sub

00:27:33.580 --> 00:27:36.730
push subscription
to an application,

00:27:36.730 --> 00:27:39.220
you're actually not
going to return 200 OK.

00:27:39.220 --> 00:27:41.530
You actually need to
return 204, which is

00:27:41.530 --> 00:27:43.177
an interesting little tidbit.

00:27:43.177 --> 00:27:45.010
But the purpose there
really is to make sure

00:27:45.010 --> 00:27:47.325
that if you return 200
OK, it's actually not

00:27:47.325 --> 00:27:48.700
going to acknowledge
the message,

00:27:48.700 --> 00:27:50.197
and it will leave
it in the queue.

00:27:50.197 --> 00:27:52.780
So you want to make sure that,
yes, you processed the message,

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:54.040
you're going to
take action, and I'm

00:27:54.040 --> 00:27:55.750
going to go ahead acknowledge
and make sure that message is

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:57.170
no longer in the Pub/Sub queue.

00:28:04.870 --> 00:28:06.790
So that's that
service, and now if we

00:28:06.790 --> 00:28:09.450
look at the messaging service,
it's actually even simpler.

00:28:15.157 --> 00:28:16.990
So same sort of boilerplate
at the top here.

00:28:16.990 --> 00:28:19.210
But a couple of
things to note here

00:28:19.210 --> 00:28:21.310
is, the first thing is
I'm going to need access

00:28:21.310 --> 00:28:23.699
to the Firebase
backend admin controls,

00:28:23.699 --> 00:28:25.240
because I'm going
to use that to send

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:27.526
the actual downstream message.

00:28:27.526 --> 00:28:30.410
And then what I'm going to do
in the process message component

00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:31.490
really is just--

00:28:31.490 --> 00:28:33.310
again, it's a push
subscription, so I just

00:28:33.310 --> 00:28:35.750
need to unpack the payload,
unpack that envelope,

00:28:35.750 --> 00:28:37.800
and pull out the
data that's in there.

00:28:37.800 --> 00:28:40.424
Make sure it says
"restock bananas,"

00:28:40.424 --> 00:28:42.590
and then go ahead and send
a message to the Firebase

00:28:42.590 --> 00:28:43.700
Messaging component.

00:28:43.700 --> 00:28:46.190
And it's actually going to
figure out where to send it to.

00:28:46.190 --> 00:28:48.470
And so that set up of
where to send it to--

00:28:48.470 --> 00:28:52.820
in this example, what I did is
I built a simple little Firebase

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:55.760
web app called "Proactively
Stocking Inventory,"

00:28:55.760 --> 00:28:58.490
and that first little bit at
the top is the instance ID.

00:28:58.490 --> 00:29:03.050
That's the specific
ID of this browser,

00:29:03.050 --> 00:29:05.750
so that it knows to send
push notifications to you.

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:07.340
And so the way I
got this up there--

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:09.680
what you would probably do
in a production scenario

00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:12.470
is store this token
inside of a database

00:29:12.470 --> 00:29:13.520
or some other component.

00:29:13.520 --> 00:29:14.978
What I did here
was I just actually

00:29:14.978 --> 00:29:18.560
put it as part of the Compute
Engine project metadata,

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:20.570
so I was able to
pull that token out

00:29:20.570 --> 00:29:23.330
and say, send it to any of
the downstream targets that

00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:24.381
are in this metadata key.

00:29:24.381 --> 00:29:26.630
So I cheated a little bit
for the purpose of the demo,

00:29:26.630 --> 00:29:28.765
but ultimately it was
able to figure out,

00:29:28.765 --> 00:29:30.140
I need to send it
to this device,

00:29:30.140 --> 00:29:31.973
and that's where the
notification came from.

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:37.440
Now what you're
probably wondering is,

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:38.450
does this really work?

00:29:38.450 --> 00:29:40.910
Did I just mock it
up, just so it does?

00:29:40.910 --> 00:29:42.780
It always says
"restock bananas."

00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:44.510
And the answer is
no, it actually

00:29:44.510 --> 00:29:46.320
does work as you'd expect.

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:50.056
So if I take another image
and send it up there--

00:29:52.912 --> 00:29:58.180
so bananas1 is actually the
first image with the full bowl.

00:29:58.180 --> 00:29:59.930
So I'm going to copy
that into the bucket.

00:29:59.930 --> 00:30:01.221
So we'll watch that go through.

00:30:05.884 --> 00:30:08.050
All right so, it's going
to copy that image up there

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:10.856
and I could show
you the logs viewer,

00:30:10.856 --> 00:30:11.980
but it's not very exciting.

00:30:11.980 --> 00:30:13.060
But effectively
what's happening here

00:30:13.060 --> 00:30:15.060
is it's making its way
through that architecture

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:17.494
and through that process,
and it's finding that nope,

00:30:17.494 --> 00:30:18.160
there's no note.

00:30:18.160 --> 00:30:20.410
There's no text that says
"restock bananas" in there,

00:30:20.410 --> 00:30:24.720
so I don't need to
send any notifications.

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:26.650
So now if we switch
back to the slides,

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:28.090
I can talk you through a
little bit of that architecture

00:30:28.090 --> 00:30:29.340
I chose for the demo purposes.

00:30:35.804 --> 00:30:36.304
Perfect.

00:30:39.424 --> 00:30:40.840
So for this one,
it actually looks

00:30:40.840 --> 00:30:43.000
relatively similar to
the production version.

00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:45.490
I made a couple of
little tweaks, primarily

00:30:45.490 --> 00:30:48.460
the use of App Engine
instead of Kubernetes Engine,

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:51.370
and I also chose to use the
stock Vision API instead

00:30:51.370 --> 00:30:54.380
of a trained custom model.

00:30:54.380 --> 00:30:57.370
And mostly because
three images is not

00:30:57.370 --> 00:31:00.260
enough to train a complex
custom model there.

00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:03.010
But ultimately, I took those
pictures with my phone.

00:31:03.010 --> 00:31:05.470
I hand-uploaded them myself
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:31:05.470 --> 00:31:07.510
And the notification
that fired back through

00:31:07.510 --> 00:31:10.790
came back through my
web browser in Chrome.

00:31:10.790 --> 00:31:13.010
But the ends-- so the
beginning and the end of that

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:16.090
are really specific
to the demo here.

00:31:16.090 --> 00:31:17.940
But if you go back
to the original,

00:31:17.940 --> 00:31:21.270
remember it's camera, local
device to do the upload,

00:31:21.270 --> 00:31:23.570
and that's actually a
phone or a handheld device

00:31:23.570 --> 00:31:25.190
on the other end from Firebase.

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:26.910
And so really there
would be no tweak

00:31:26.910 --> 00:31:28.340
I'd have to do with most
of the architecture,

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:29.960
because everything
on the Cloud side

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:32.210
is actually pretty well set
up like you would normally

00:31:32.210 --> 00:31:32.894
want it to work.

00:31:32.894 --> 00:31:35.060
It's just a matter of
changing the upload, the input

00:31:35.060 --> 00:31:38.370
segment, and which device
you're actually targeting.

00:31:38.370 --> 00:31:39.890
And those Firebase
messages actually

00:31:39.890 --> 00:31:43.370
have the ability to be targeted
across multiple devices, not

00:31:43.370 --> 00:31:44.450
just a single.

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:46.550
So you're not sending
a push notification

00:31:46.550 --> 00:31:48.140
to every single team member.

00:31:48.140 --> 00:31:51.840
You may have all of your folks
running with a custom-built app

00:31:51.840 --> 00:31:52.904
there.

00:31:52.904 --> 00:31:54.320
And you may say,
well, I just want

00:31:54.320 --> 00:31:56.111
to send it to the people
who are subscribed

00:31:56.111 --> 00:31:56.992
to inventory updates.

00:31:56.992 --> 00:31:59.450
So now you have the ability to
actually specifically target

00:31:59.450 --> 00:32:02.740
those that are ready to run back
to the room and restock bananas

00:32:02.740 --> 00:32:03.871
and fill them in.

00:32:07.030 --> 00:32:10.520
So where do you go from here?

00:32:10.520 --> 00:32:13.010
So streaming-- and I mentioned
this a little up front,

00:32:13.010 --> 00:32:14.810
but I think it's
worth revisiting.

00:32:14.810 --> 00:32:17.860
I think streaming is an
interesting approach.

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:20.500
Instead of sampling,
taking that raw footage

00:32:20.500 --> 00:32:22.450
and just throwing
it up to the Cloud.

00:32:22.450 --> 00:32:24.490
And maybe using
instead of Vision API,

00:32:24.490 --> 00:32:27.460
using the Video
Intelligence API.

00:32:27.460 --> 00:32:30.780
The APIs are actually relatively
similar in that the Video

00:32:30.780 --> 00:32:33.130
Intelligence API functions
much like the Vision API

00:32:33.130 --> 00:32:36.516
does, except it's looking at
moving images instead of--

00:32:36.516 --> 00:32:38.890
looking at it frame-by-frame
within a large set of images

00:32:38.890 --> 00:32:40.420
as opposed to a single image.

00:32:40.420 --> 00:32:43.960
But it effectively functions the
same way in terms of the label,

00:32:43.960 --> 00:32:47.130
object, face detection--
that sort of thing.

00:32:47.130 --> 00:32:49.400
But you're back to, is
it worth the investment

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:50.300
to stream that data?

00:32:50.300 --> 00:32:52.049
Do you need to have
the network bandwidth?

00:32:52.049 --> 00:32:54.500
Do you need to have
a more capable device

00:32:54.500 --> 00:32:57.290
on prem or in the store to
actually send that data up?

00:32:57.290 --> 00:32:58.520
It might not be, right?

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:00.300
Depending on the
size and the scope

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:02.300
of the deployment you're
trying to put together,

00:33:02.300 --> 00:33:05.380
it might not be an
appropriate fit.

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:07.240
Well, what about
shelf-based inventory?

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:10.510
How do we see all the way
through the back of a shelf?

00:33:10.510 --> 00:33:12.466
We could with
shelf-mounted cameras,

00:33:12.466 --> 00:33:13.840
and it's actually
a great partner

00:33:13.840 --> 00:33:16.881
of ours, Trax, who's over in
the retail showcase in Moscone

00:33:16.881 --> 00:33:17.380
West.

00:33:17.380 --> 00:33:19.930
It actually has
shelf-based cameras

00:33:19.930 --> 00:33:21.181
exactly for this purpose.

00:33:21.181 --> 00:33:22.930
So they've actually
got a really cool demo

00:33:22.930 --> 00:33:26.410
of stacks of boxes of donuts.

00:33:26.410 --> 00:33:27.910
And every time you
pull a donut out,

00:33:27.910 --> 00:33:29.710
it updates the inventory count.

00:33:29.710 --> 00:33:32.350
So that actually could work
for certain other use cases

00:33:32.350 --> 00:33:35.590
where again, they're
narrowly packed in items.

00:33:35.590 --> 00:33:37.930
You can't get overhead
cameras in appropriately.

00:33:37.930 --> 00:33:39.820
They've got a good little
shelf-based system,

00:33:39.820 --> 00:33:41.582
and it functions in
a lot of similar ways

00:33:41.582 --> 00:33:42.790
to what I've shown you today.

00:33:45.300 --> 00:33:46.955
What about a custom model?

00:33:46.955 --> 00:33:49.080
What if you want to go with
something that we can't

00:33:49.080 --> 00:33:52.545
do with Vision API or AutoML?

00:33:52.545 --> 00:33:55.527
What if you need something
incredibly more specific?

00:33:55.527 --> 00:33:57.860
Well, that's where you can
use something like TensorFlow

00:33:57.860 --> 00:34:02.210
to build a very specific
custom object detection model,

00:34:02.210 --> 00:34:06.690
and you actually run that on top
of our Cloud ML Engine Service.

00:34:06.690 --> 00:34:08.600
And so what the Cloud
ML Engine Service

00:34:08.600 --> 00:34:12.500
gives you is the ability to
train in incredible scale,

00:34:12.500 --> 00:34:15.050
and do things like
hyperparameter tuning, which

00:34:15.050 --> 00:34:17.719
lets you specify which
parameters in your model

00:34:17.719 --> 00:34:20.360
do you want to tweak, and how
many permutations do you want

00:34:20.360 --> 00:34:21.500
us to run them through?

00:34:21.500 --> 00:34:22.916
So we can actually
take your model

00:34:22.916 --> 00:34:25.429
and say, well let's
run 500 permutations,

00:34:25.429 --> 00:34:28.070
tweaking each of those
individual parameters,

00:34:28.070 --> 00:34:30.560
and figure out which mix is
the right mix of parameters

00:34:30.560 --> 00:34:32.630
for your model to
be most effective.

00:34:32.630 --> 00:34:34.469
And that's actually
baked into the service.

00:34:34.469 --> 00:34:35.929
And then when it's
done training,

00:34:35.929 --> 00:34:38.159
you can actually promote
that model to production,

00:34:38.159 --> 00:34:41.480
and we give you a nice
little prediction endpoint

00:34:41.480 --> 00:34:44.219
that we run in scale
for you as needed.

00:34:44.219 --> 00:34:46.400
And that prediction
endpoint can be integrated

00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:48.765
into any application.

00:34:48.765 --> 00:34:49.639
So that's one option.

00:34:49.639 --> 00:34:52.834
And then if you need to get that
machine learning work really

00:34:52.834 --> 00:34:54.500
close to the edge,
for whatever reason--

00:34:54.500 --> 00:34:56.389
maybe you've got a
very custom use case.

00:34:56.389 --> 00:34:59.210
Maybe you want to layer in
some very specific detection,

00:34:59.210 --> 00:35:02.590
and the latency to Cloud is
just a little bit too high.

00:35:02.590 --> 00:35:04.670
That certainly could happen.

00:35:04.670 --> 00:35:07.220
Well, running machine
learning models at the edge--

00:35:07.220 --> 00:35:10.730
running TensorFlow models
or hosted TensorFlow--

00:35:10.730 --> 00:35:12.680
I would say it's not a
non-trivial operation.

00:35:12.680 --> 00:35:15.720
It does require a
fair amount of work.

00:35:15.720 --> 00:35:17.840
So we have a package
actually called Kubeflow,

00:35:17.840 --> 00:35:19.610
which takes all of the
components you need

00:35:19.610 --> 00:35:22.460
to run machine learning models
and host them, and actually

00:35:22.460 --> 00:35:25.340
even update them there, and
run it on top of any Kubernetes

00:35:25.340 --> 00:35:26.077
cluster.

00:35:26.077 --> 00:35:28.160
So you could imagine, if
you've got infrastructure

00:35:28.160 --> 00:35:31.130
inside the store and you can
deploy Kubernetes on there,

00:35:31.130 --> 00:35:33.740
you could actually take
those trained models

00:35:33.740 --> 00:35:35.270
and just push them
down to the store

00:35:35.270 --> 00:35:37.880
and run them in that
environment inside of Kubeflow.

00:35:37.880 --> 00:35:40.527
And it includes Notebook
Infrastructure, TensorBoard,

00:35:40.527 --> 00:35:42.110
and a bunch of other
tools so that you

00:35:42.110 --> 00:35:45.185
can update those models
in flight if you need to.

00:35:45.185 --> 00:35:47.560
And the last point, which is
not on here, because it does

00:35:47.560 --> 00:35:48.532
come up--

00:35:48.532 --> 00:35:50.240
I'm going to go back
a handful of images,

00:35:50.240 --> 00:35:51.406
because I want to show you--

00:35:51.406 --> 00:35:54.880
is this approach that I
showed today is actually based

00:35:54.880 --> 00:36:00.000
on stock being there,
less, and then gone.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:01.800
But there's still
some amount of time

00:36:01.800 --> 00:36:03.822
it would take for
that notification

00:36:03.822 --> 00:36:06.030
to make it through, even if
it's a couple of seconds.

00:36:06.030 --> 00:36:07.740
That's still time
for someone to have

00:36:07.740 --> 00:36:10.380
to go, get the notification,
look at their phone,

00:36:10.380 --> 00:36:14.130
acknowledge it, go back to
the back room, fill up a cart,

00:36:14.130 --> 00:36:16.170
and then restock the
actual inventory.

00:36:16.170 --> 00:36:18.840
So this might not actually
work for a lot of you, right?

00:36:18.840 --> 00:36:20.800
That's where
something like AutoML

00:36:20.800 --> 00:36:24.000
can really make the difference,
because you may supply images

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:26.490
like this and say, well,
for that middle one,

00:36:26.490 --> 00:36:30.000
we're going to label images
like that as "low stock."

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:32.850
And maybe you want the
notifications being generated

00:36:32.850 --> 00:36:35.960
by "low stock," not "no stock."

00:36:35.960 --> 00:36:38.960
So that kind of
distinction is really

00:36:38.960 --> 00:36:40.970
only possible with
something like AutoML.

00:36:40.970 --> 00:36:43.850
Where we need to get a little
bit more fine grain than just

00:36:43.850 --> 00:36:46.370
general detection, you want to
add a little bit more context

00:36:46.370 --> 00:36:48.410
to what you're seeing, and
that's where some like AutoML

00:36:48.410 --> 00:36:49.409
can make a difference.

00:36:49.409 --> 00:36:51.200
So that specific kind
of training approach.

00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:57.390
So where do you go from here?

00:36:57.390 --> 00:36:59.340
What's the first
thing you should do?

00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:02.310
I urge all of you, if you have
not had a chance to play within

00:37:02.310 --> 00:37:06.450
Vision API, to go to
cloud.google.com/vision

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:08.370
and start uploading images.

00:37:08.370 --> 00:37:10.826
There's a nice little box on
there and on that page where

00:37:10.826 --> 00:37:11.950
you can just drag and drop.

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:13.890
It's a nice little
HTML5 uploader.

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:15.990
Drag and drop an image
on there, and see

00:37:15.990 --> 00:37:17.490
all of the data
that comes back out.

00:37:17.490 --> 00:37:18.940
It's incredible.

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:22.200
You can see every single
thing, and if you remember back

00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:24.535
to those images of the
banana in the bowl,

00:37:24.535 --> 00:37:26.160
when I did those
results and I probably

00:37:26.160 --> 00:37:28.284
should have pasted them in
here, what's really cool

00:37:28.284 --> 00:37:31.020
is it told me it
had wood in there,

00:37:31.020 --> 00:37:32.712
and it had a reasonably
high confidence

00:37:32.712 --> 00:37:34.170
that there was wood
in the picture,

00:37:34.170 --> 00:37:35.550
as well as the bananas in there.

00:37:35.550 --> 00:37:37.470
It actually was able
to read the stickers

00:37:37.470 --> 00:37:39.090
and tell me what
was on the sticker.

00:37:39.090 --> 00:37:41.130
And it gave me not
only the amount

00:37:41.130 --> 00:37:44.759
that it saw of the colors
in both those objects

00:37:44.759 --> 00:37:46.800
that it found, and it also
gave me the confidence

00:37:46.800 --> 00:37:50.320
that it had that those were
the actual individual objects.

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:52.080
So it gets really,
really specific,

00:37:52.080 --> 00:37:54.114
and it's a really powerful tool.

00:37:54.114 --> 00:37:55.530
And it's actually
capable of doing

00:37:55.530 --> 00:37:58.630
things like specific
brand detection as well.

00:37:58.630 --> 00:38:00.690
So if you take a picture
of the Starbucks logo

00:38:00.690 --> 00:38:01.860
and you upload it,
it will actually

00:38:01.860 --> 00:38:04.560
tell you without even seeing the
words that it's the Starbucks

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:07.890
logo, or the Nike swoosh
on the side of a shoe.

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:10.020
So it's a pretty
incredible capable API,

00:38:10.020 --> 00:38:13.110
because it's been trained
across the library of images

00:38:13.110 --> 00:38:16.436
that Google has access to.

00:38:16.436 --> 00:38:18.060
So that's the thing
I would start with.

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:19.436
Go through that,
upload an image,

00:38:19.436 --> 00:38:21.976
look through all the results,
look through what you can find,

00:38:21.976 --> 00:38:23.730
and throw crazy
things at it, right?

00:38:23.730 --> 00:38:25.536
Because it's a lot
of fun to see what

00:38:25.536 --> 00:38:26.910
you might be able
to build-- what

00:38:26.910 --> 00:38:29.700
you may able to do with the data
that comes out of those images.

00:38:29.700 --> 00:38:31.680
There's so much hidden
in there that I think

00:38:31.680 --> 00:38:33.801
we might take for granted.

00:38:33.801 --> 00:38:36.380
The second thing I would
have you do coming out

00:38:36.380 --> 00:38:37.970
of this session
is check out some

00:38:37.970 --> 00:38:40.430
of these other ones that are
coming up today and tomorrow.

00:38:40.430 --> 00:38:42.700
So "Injecting AI into
your Applications."

00:38:42.700 --> 00:38:47.120
Then gets more specific around
each of the pre-trained machine

00:38:47.120 --> 00:38:51.330
learning APIs that we
have as part of GCP.

00:38:51.330 --> 00:38:54.450
The Vision session actually
goes into specifically the stock

00:38:54.450 --> 00:38:58.540
Vision API as well as the power
that Cloud AutoML gives it,

00:38:58.540 --> 00:39:01.920
and where you can
supercharge it.

00:39:01.920 --> 00:39:03.850
Then there's also
"Intelligent Image Analytics

00:39:03.850 --> 00:39:06.490
Infrastructure," and then
that goes a level deeper

00:39:06.490 --> 00:39:09.175
into more and more
of those use cases.

00:39:09.175 --> 00:39:11.300
And then the last one is
"Hybrid Machine Learning."

00:39:11.300 --> 00:39:13.460
That actually talks
a lot about Kubeflow

00:39:13.460 --> 00:39:15.830
and running in
Kubernetes environments,

00:39:15.830 --> 00:39:17.960
whether at the edge or
in your own data center.

00:39:17.960 --> 00:39:20.810
But it goes into, what does that
machine learning lifecycle look

00:39:20.810 --> 00:39:21.400
like?

00:39:21.400 --> 00:39:23.727
What if you wanted
to train on the Cloud

00:39:23.727 --> 00:39:26.060
and then take that trained
model and run it at the edge?

00:39:26.060 --> 00:39:27.810
Or run it in your data center?

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:30.227
So we give you
those capabilities.

00:39:30.227 --> 00:39:32.310
So those are some of the
sessions I would strongly

00:39:32.310 --> 00:39:34.856
urge you to check out.

00:39:34.856 --> 00:39:36.230
And the last thing
I would say is

00:39:36.230 --> 00:39:37.670
